#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064992} :activity/id #uuid "8a6775e9-2229-5837-a376-b69fe905d97e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048123 :topic/id #uuid "80ecc4ad-075a-51a6-a1fc-72dc61a2444d" :topic/math-academy-id 313} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065347 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,7]$ shown below.\n\n![](images/39/39851b8fc5658ccfd92711eb9fe7a00a730300a3bf2e635911bdd51ea69de37a.png)\n\nThe **critical points** of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=4,$ there is a sharp corner, so $f'(x)$ doesn't exist.\n\nSo, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=2$ and $x=4.$ \n\nIn particular, a critical point is called a **stationary point** if $f'(x)=0.$ So here, the points $x=2$ and $x=4$ are both critical points, but only the point at $x=2$ is a stationary point.\n\n![](images/4e/4e91994fa85a068ef847cbd82dc176569fd15ab2487dfff6936280e9a1deb634.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "36584e6c-f116-5ad4-bb03-576b0f7e9c0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02673d5b-6611-5854-bca0-ff7445f2f3c3" :step/math-academy-id 12782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064044 :question/id #uuid "2d9fc36f-8d8f-5e57-9ff0-28a032e71980" :question/math-academy-id "e-3726"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402909}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "2b2aa51f-4124-5395-afb4-05149fddd969" :step/math-academy-id 12789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064116 :question/id #uuid "97897d09-3dc9-5e35-8f56-d44f54c542cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6169"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514466}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "589482bf-a622-576c-8b59-1c5332c13087" :step/math-academy-id 23042 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065374 :tutorial/content "Some critical points are **local extrema**. There are two types of local extrema:\n\n- A **local minimum** is the lowest point of a valley in a graph. Equivalently, $x=a$ is a local minimum if for $x$ values \"close\" to $x=a,$ we have $f(x) \\geq f(a)$.\n\n- A **local maximum** is the highest point of a peak in a graph. Equivalently, $x=a$ is a local maximum if for $x$ values \"close\" to $x=a,$ we have $f(x) \\leq f(a)$.\n\n![](images/d6/d6fbdf74b282bd5b8172fcc7927f84bbdfd050a717794f3b40c8e58b83d7b87e.png)\n\nOn the graph above, the critical point at $x=4$ is a local minimum since it is the lowest point of a valley. Likewise, the critical point at $x=6$ is a local maximum since it is the highest point of a peak. But the critical point at $x=2$ is not a local extremum since it is neither a peak nor a valley.\n\nThe **global extrema** of a function in an interval are given by the lowest or highest points of the function's graph in the whole interval.\n\n- The lowest point is the **global minimum**, whereas\n\n- the highest point is the **global maximum**.\n\nHere, the global minimum is at $x=4$, and the global maximum is at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/8a/8adb83da866dae1cd122b5271b2cb9036c72a389eaf1c48148888140bda576ac.png)\n\n\"Local\" extrema are the lowest or highest points compared to points that are very close to them. On the other hand, \"global\" extrema are the lowest/highest points of the graph over its entire domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "05856315-caeb-577d-9dea-b5e5f8830820" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3898 :tutorial/title "Local and Global Extrema of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "b952eadc-3fe2-5be0-8961-f9af3dc6fcd5" :step/math-academy-id 23043 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064993}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064409}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065007} :activity/id #uuid "22bb5766-c513-5d78-a9f8-52a5e929a9f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754 :topic/id #uuid "bb9926b3-3427-50e2-94a5-96b4e07466cd" :topic/math-academy-id 312} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065407 :tutorial/content "Remember that the exponential function $e^x$ has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn't change anything, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right)  = e^ x.\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d2a6d036-0881-5a58-8935-905cd004a7e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fc7af3af-558f-5f0f-9b14-e5c45f77ad79" :step/math-academy-id 1369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064049 :question/id #uuid "3ff03590-c618-5a60-b2ac-e0a20be6a8ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9548e6-28ef-5a97-ab04-dfb1b6e23c16" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339013}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function"} :step/id #uuid "3b0b5798-a45c-58ad-a6a5-18ca5ba2df53" :step/math-academy-id 1370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065408 :tutorial/content "Remember that if $a$ is a positive real number, then the derivative of $a^x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x}\\left( a^ x\\right)\t=\ta^ x \\ln a.\n$$\n\nSo, by \"reversing\" this process, we obtain the following formula for the integral of a general exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int  a^x \\, \\textrm d x  = \\dfrac{a^ x }{\\ln a} +C.\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we wish to calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x   &= \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + C\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "254d6a12-01a6-5a3b-b0fd-fbefc017a149" :tutorial/math-academy-id 714 :tutorial/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"} :step/id #uuid "51842b79-1471-58e3-8240-0e6f8c062fda" :step/math-academy-id 1374 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064050 :question/id #uuid "66cbcaeb-93ee-5c15-a230-e66d2fbdd761" :question/math-academy-id "e-380"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bd8c6d8-0b11-5735-88a1-9b12e6315401" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297341}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Exponential Function With a General Base"} :step/id #uuid "6e6c41bb-fda0-558d-bff8-d7d5bc882df6" :step/math-academy-id 1377 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064051 :question/id #uuid "90010ae5-e211-510f-b188-dc3006926d94" :question/math-academy-id "e-381"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488378} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514423}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c64b94e8-f57d-5360-9a8d-ce365fb8c43d" :step/math-academy-id 1379}] :activity/title "Integrating Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064408}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017} :activity/id #uuid "313df706-051c-53f8-a9d2-078969d05bee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777 :topic/id #uuid "bdc990ad-e824-598d-8c5d-0b62dd6e1501" :topic/math-academy-id 285} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065409 :tutorial/content "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}x+C \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." :tutorial/id #uuid "bfa0c70d-6cf7-5c69-8995-d9f6c980a4ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "49d02f63-57e0-5168-80dd-fdc3893de9af" :step/math-academy-id 1381 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064052 :question/id #uuid "a9671d61-6b85-5d84-b49c-a6443569f6c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-382"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479280} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514384}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "e743bf3f-f489-55bd-a78e-d650597e4e7c" :step/math-academy-id 1382 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064053 :question/id #uuid "4c3342aa-dd1c-566b-9229-164e594c554c" :question/math-aca