step/id #uuid "1eb256c9-e635-52b0-9693-979de7534909" :step/math-academy-id 1387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064055 :question/id #uuid "c2c57145-3988-5ce8-b13f-114bba6964f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9836abbe-e172-5c2f-8b19-145ef6d5c02d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479237} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479228}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"} :step/id #uuid "ffd7ef8c-69c3-5ccb-ac1c-da42a0605f1c" :step/math-academy-id 1389}] :activity/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064392}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425} :activity/id #uuid "0693eaa1-2cbe-58a3-aa5d-c539aa74793e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/id #uuid "c2580f8e-3ba3-5aa9-b1f1-0d8e49986381" :topic/math-academy-id 1109} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097818 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{'}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{'} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{'} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" :tutorial/id #uuid "958f67f2-8e00-5818-8af7-9f88561ef84c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2622 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef3171cb-fb23-5d4d-8819-73da9533de18" :step/math-academy-id 12327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299752}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"} :step/id #uuid "e27ef05d-bc90-5cce-9885-8f21279044e8" :step/math-academy-id 12328 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "6cbd3b19-1c1f-519e-b232-315484ae9234" :step/math-academy-id 12329 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097819 :tutorial/content "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf $y = u(x) \\cdot v(x),$ then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot  v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c6d5393-f91c-57e5-999c-92eedf550f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2623 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "4a3a2bef-3f64-510c-8bf8-25d81169d680" :step/math-academy-id 12330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514464}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "138782c6-ad9e-5b08-b3dd-375476961e1e" :step/math-academy-id 12331}] :activity/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096889}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430} :activity/id #uuid "a699a755-f56a-5a6a-8493-60b83f42eaa5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842 :topic/id #uuid "c54eaff0-4e6f-57d2-aa08-401a88379d23" :topic/math-academy-id 1110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097820 :tutorial/content "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x).$ \n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=x^2+1.$  Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(x)^{'}=1, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dd2362a7-73d2-577f-aa31-2150f8f6b63f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ed5427f5-4392-5166-a825-dd9dcb07cc3d" :step/math-academy-id 12332 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :question/id #uuid "932c9b44-c040-5ae1-84f7-277767c255d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492115} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314926}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "6859d258-9c69-5bab-88e5-4b946166e00d" :step/math-academy-id 12333 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :question/id #uuid "f804809a-22bf-5ab3-9775-3591d4581797" :question/math-academy-id "e-3638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488448} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514422}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f384d7ca-2c43-5aae-b786-23e8a903bb4f" :step/math-academy-id 12334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097821 :tutorial/content "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x}\n- u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ed29747-9007-50b8-9347-89e46eabc182" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2625 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "185d55a4-49d5-53d5-95b3-8eb93729ad7f" :step/math-academy-id 12335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :question/id #uuid "53d435ea-a20f-5216-8020-f36cdf8b7c36" :question/math-academy-id "e-3639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329848}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "f7097f26-3f43-5e1d-8496-783599f1b7ac" :step/math-academy-id 12336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097822 :tutorial/content "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}x+{sin}^{2}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "584707ea-2c96-5389-926d-b9ac87adc17c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2626 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "c001eb53-21b0-5423-a50f-1e506d151f8c" :step/math-academy-id 12337}] :activity/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096890}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093327} :activity/id #uuid "bfd96d16-131a-5887-9f3c-822479cc8e6b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119 :topic/id #uuid "dc19c9ce-6319-5943-8842-352e3c3735c7" :topic/math-academy-id 987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092083 :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/math-academy-id "e-6080"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493326} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514424}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "978ac109-c615-5055-9e0a-f164ca3a1990" :step/math-academy-id 22383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093442 :tutorial/content "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{1cm} m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}.\n$$\n\nHere, $m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}$ because the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line, and $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $A(1,1).$ This normal line is the dashed line shown below, which is perpendicular to the solid tangent line. \n\n![](images/31/31ba09ff14e5bd485794f283c5d6a5b087b6c21fdb9242af01b75ec8e8744e0d.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nAt $A(1,1),$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nf'(1) \t= 2(1) = 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, we want the normal line, and the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(1)} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we work out the equation of the normal line using the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the normal line is $y=-\\dfrac x 2 + \\dfrac 3 2.$ We could also write this in standard form as $x+2y=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c999b-ac30-5116-acbd-795f13cc2b3d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 359 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5a4d7867-7ef2-52ad-93ae-c3ea0c556954" :step/math-academy-id 667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091953 :question/id #uuid "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480110} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338957}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "ee29e21c-ec79-5cef-8080-e39f4ded4176" :step/math-academy-id 668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091954 :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/math-academy-id "e-149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493324} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493321}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"} :step/id #uuid "434b8ac4-0cfe-5365-987b-8d6de7b04e04" :step/math-academy-id 671}] :activity/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092461}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079227} :activity/id #uuid "c69d16f5-44d0-5069-b44e-d2caf86ea506" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049806 :topic/id #uuid "156024ea-0497-5260-b6dc-995afd569210" :topic/math-academy-id 638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079683 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/e4/e46fcaf79d93513c8b946de51ef451ad8a638936681ce3fc70ffdbe8de023078.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(3{x}^{2}+kx) \\\\  & =3(-3)^{2}+k(-3) \\\\  & =27-3k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =(-3)^{3} \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3k & =-27 \\\\ 3k & =54 \\\\ k & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3,$ we need to choose $k=18.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "10dabe2c-998a-5731-bcf2-c0ed93bc7f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 543 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "778b28a2-a45c-54f3-993a-34eba4c8d1b4" :step/math-academy-id 1021 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079229}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079228 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078220 :question/id #uuid "d17b0e14-aabc-5814-a242-d5fb803cb406" :question/math-academy-id "e-263"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/key-prere