{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507525}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "31a192d6-435a-5d0f-9c21-a362b860c2c8" :step/math-academy-id 1027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078334 :question/id #uuid "aef91e7a-138c-58bc-a25c-ca77284581c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6279"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507479}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"} :step/id #uuid "5f820e0e-c43c-5112-8821-d68f8ea15c85" :step/math-academy-id 23595 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079230}}] :activity/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078661}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093116} :activity/id #uuid "9ed557a5-e06f-5f5a-9230-32a93a80b727" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/id #uuid "3b427f21-e66e-500e-b1b5-ede02904d802" :topic/math-academy-id 1007} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093369 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can be used to differentiate exponential functions like \n\n$$\ny = e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u = 3x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=3x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf67b8fb-2658-5221-b309-6cf09e93b5e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1268 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "87b44f73-3af6-53c8-a8c8-61525491b5d4" :step/math-academy-id 2491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329902}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"} :step/id #uuid "a179fdba-d910-50b0-81ea-369c06993adf" :step/math-academy-id 2492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329903}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "a8a87a08-8a33-5f7d-a2ee-3bb4f46035fe" :step/math-academy-id 2495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514385}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"} :step/id #uuid "e3bd15e6-53e0-51f6-bb04-9b4ea73b76c4" :step/math-academy-id 2498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093118}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092399}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060528} :activity/id #uuid "9be3f59b-09db-52f4-97b2-cfb6afd98870" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420 :topic/id #uuid "476d32c8-5b16-54ef-9688-daca6c7937ad" :topic/math-academy-id 280} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060893 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "dca4e1b4-5f89-5c88-9f24-b4671410d644" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2618 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "970c03a9-20ec-54a6-81c1-0b04852bff2d" :step/math-academy-id 12320 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059503 :question/id #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" :question/math-academy-id "e-3631"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489497} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514433}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "727b4e4a-8b24-5865-8b3f-cc8d94213ab1" :step/math-academy-id 12321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :question/id #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3632"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483539} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483538}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"} :step/id #uuid "3ba7adb6-6e48-5f3e-af46-a386f761686f" :step/math-academy-id 12322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060894 :tutorial/content "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)+\\operatorname{sin}(h)\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a25dc2a-846b-5554-9cc7-682b81f17e0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2619 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Sine"} :step/id #uuid "58bef47e-2b3d-546b-87f7-1097147174c7" :step/math-academy-id 12324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060895 :tutorial/content "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)-\\operatorname{cos}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." :tutorial/id #uuid "40bcba62-6d88-5cfd-9d89-e773514e5a63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2620 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "eb4d0827-6392-5186-8463-5ccc6e4e3392" :step/math-academy-id 12325 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060896 :tutorial/content "We can also prove that $(\\tan x)'= \\sec^2 x$ using limits, though it's a bit cumbersome. \n\nA faster way is to use a rule of differentiation known as the **quotient rule**. \n\nYou'll learn about the quotient rule very soon!" :tutorial/id #uuid "32a1aa14-0e0a-53c1-a40c-e728e4b231c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2621 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "2b5a3334-fa23-5bc8-bf82-7d80f190e65d" :step/math-academy-id 12326}] :activity/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059951}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534} :activity/id #uuid "10bdaba2-b2d6-5479-a66a-9f5c1d933300" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902 :topic/id #uuid "6fa843c3-0803-5725-a7d6-d567e4f1b216" :topic/math-academy-id 281} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060897 :tutorial/content "The **second derivative**  is the derivative of a function's first derivative. It is written as $f''(x).$\n\nTo calculate the second derivative of a function $f(x)$, we first need to calculate the first derivative $f'(x).$ After that, we calculate the derivative of $f'(x).$ So using prime $\\left('\\right)$ notation, we can define the second derivative of a function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = (f'(x))'.\n$$\n\nLet's try it out with the function $f(x) = 4x^2 + 3x.$ The first derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}+3x)^{'} \\\\  & =4(2)x+3 \\\\  & =8x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$ to give us $f''(x).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =({f}^{'}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(8x+3)^{'} \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the second derivative of $f(x)$ is $8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "947c462e-76d9-5248-93f8-a558f1ad16c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9931d327-3dfa-546d-9bad-52bfcf096bda" :step/math-academy-id 12357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059505 :question/id #uuid "0bc72287-b782-5add-89ef-f2a465573ab0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3653"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514409}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "67b86b8b-be5c-54ba-b03c-e422581c1992" :step/math-academy-id 12358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060898 :tutorial/content "We can also express the second derivative using $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}$ notation, also known as Leibniz notation. If we have a function $f(x),$ then the second derivative $f''(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 f}{\\textrm d x^2} = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm df} {\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSometimes, we define a function as $y=f(x),$ in which case we could also express the second derivative as $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f21f7901-ec61-5c3d-a6f2-6bba03a55a17" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2634 :tutorial/title "Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "8fa38988-5cde-5291-bf68-d15bba187f2a" :step/math-academy-id 12359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :question/id #uuid "b092bc79-d369-5ff0-a6bb-5fc059699275" :question/math-academy-id "e-3654"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484417} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484415}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "65dcc063-d790-5cae-ba88-b2b94d516359" :step/math-academy-id 12360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060899 :tutorial/content "To calculate the third derivative of the function $f(x)=2x^4+3x,$ we need to differentiate it three times.\n\nComputing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}+3x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1}+1\\cdot 3{x}^{1-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =8{x}^{3}+3{x}^{0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}+3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3{x}^{3-1}+0 \\\\ \\text{ } & =24{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot 2{x}^{2-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third derivative of $f(x)$ is $f'''(x)=48x.$ \n\nUsing Leibniz notation, the third derivative of a function $f(x)$ can also be written as\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(f(x))\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^3}{\\textrm d x^3}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn general, if we want to calculate the $n$-th order derivative of a function, we need to differentiate it $n$ times:\n\n$$\nf^{(n)}(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}f(x).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The parenthesis in the superscript of $f^{(n)}(x)$ means that it is the the $n$-th derivative of $f(x).$ This is to distinguish it from $f^n(x)$ with no parenthesis, which indicates the $n$-th power of $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bfdba2-25dc-51af-97f8-00b3b216d0a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2635 :tutorial/title "Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "2c85fed7-69ed-56ea-8cfa-78cf775d9d35" :step/math-academy-id 12361 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059507 :question/id #uuid "627f1ef4-64a8-5764-89c7-cb4e31009dbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3655"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317359}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "75f9df48-9a05-5828-82cb-52a03412297c" :step/math-academy-id 12362}] :activity/title "Second and Higher-Order Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059952}]}
